WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.417
Coding Exercise 4 asks you to update one existing patient by a stable key.

00:00:05.417 --> 00:00:09.397
The medical record number identifies the intended patient.

00:00:09.397 --> 00:00:12.232
The new age is given in the task.

00:00:12.232 --> 00:00:17.952
Before writing the UPDATE, imagine the preview SELECT you would run in a real workflow.

00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:22.112
It should return exactly one row for that medical record number.

00:00:22.112 --> 00:00:28.032
Then complete the SET and WHERE parts and use RETURNING to inspect the changed row.

00:00:28.032 --> 00:00:30.037
Run and Check.

00:00:30.037 --> 00:00:35.622
If more than one row could be affected by your condition, the condition is not safe enough.

00:00:35.622 --> 00:00:38.422
If PostgreSQL updates no rows, verify the key value.

00:00:38.422 --> 00:00:46.752
This exercise is about targeting the correct record as much as it is about the UPDATE syntax.

00:00:46.752 --> 00:00:50.912
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:50.912 --> 00:00:55.977
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:55.977 --> 00:00:59.537
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:59.537 --> 00:01:03.677
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:01:03.677 --> 00:01:10.737
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:14.907
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:01:14.907 --> 00:01:19.615
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
